 "path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-openclaw-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out-3/play/users/u066/system/state/openclaw/agents/main/agent/workshop-skills/arc-rectangle-noise/SKILL.md",
 "edits": [
  {
   "oldText": "description: Solve ARC grids with rectangular frames and enclosed regions, or noisy solid rectangular fields; infer fills and colors from demonstrations.",
   "newText": "description: Infer ARC transformations on rectangular frames, enclosed interiors, or noisy solid rectangular fields, including qualifying fills and recolors."
  },
  {
   "oldText": "1. Compare input and output cell by cell. Identify solid fields, frame boundaries, enclosed interiors, anomalous pixels, and any repeated or aligned patterns; distinguish a hollow frame from a noisy solid field. Record every changed cell and relevant unchanged controls rather than narrating tentative interpretations.\n2. Infer the exact condition for each change: which structure, color, position, alignment, or relation qualifies, and the replacement value. Check the rule against all demonstrated changes and invariants, including apparent exceptions; do not extrapolate from one changed cell or infer a broad fill/recolor rule from a sparse example. If competing rules remain, request another demo.\n3. Apply only a rule that accounts for every demo change to the test grid. Preserve all cells not selected by the verified condition, then check dimensions and each changed/unchanged cell before submitting. When demos do not establish a rule, request a demo instead of submitting a guess.",
   "newText": "1. Compare input and output cell by cell. Identify rectangular solid fields, frame boundaries, enclosed interiors, anomalous pixels, and repeated/aligned patterns; distinguish a hollow frame from a noisy solid field. Record changed cells and unchanged controls.\n2. Infer the exact qualifying structure, color, position, alignment, or relation and replacement value. Check every change and invariant, including apparent exceptions. Do not generalize a fill/recolor rule from sparse evidence; if competing rules remain, use the demo-request procedure in `arc-stream-actions`.\n3. Apply only a rule that explains all demos. Preserve cells outside its condition and verify dimensions and each changed/unchanged cell. For output construction and final checks, follow `arc-stream-actions`."
  }
 ]
}

{
 "path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-openclaw-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out-3/play/users/u066/system/state/openclaw/agents/main/agent/workshop-skills/arc-stream-actions/SKILL.md",
 "edits": [
  {
   "oldText": "1. Use only the current instance's grid, demos, feedback, and counters; treat instances as independent and do not inspect other task artifacts. Identify the transformation by comparing every input/output cell, dimensions, preserved regions, and changed cells. Follow `arc-divider-recolor` for divider-panel tasks and `arc-rectangle-noise` for rectangular frames/fields. An unchanged demo constrains only that example; it does not establish the general rule or prove the test is unchanged. If the test lacks an apparent divider or other defining structure seen in demos, do not force it into that task family or guess a split; request a demo to establish how the transformation applies.\n2. Request a demo when competing rules remain, choosing the one most likely to distinguish them and tracking its cost/cap. Never infer a precise mapping from visual resemblance, an apparent color histogram, or stochastic-looking areas. If demonstrated output contains apparently random/permuted content with no rule to generate its exact values, further samples do not license guessing: distinguish the fixed structure from the undetermined cells, and only submit if the applicable scoring task admits a valid constrained completion; otherwise use the remaining request budget to investigate structural relations, not to invent a random seed.\n3. Before submitting, require one rule to account for all changed and preserved cells in every demo. Construct the exact output dimensions and values, then verify it mechanically: count rows and cells per row, compare the set of non-background coordinates against the input, and check each output value against the rule. For sparse grids, enumerate marked coordinates row by row before building the output; do not rely on visual memory or casually retype a long grid. After incorrect feedback, discard the proposed output, do not infer which cells/rule were wrong from binary rejection, and request a discriminating demo rather than cycling through guesses. Track request/attempt caps and costs. `finish` is permitted only once the instance is over.\n4. On every response, output exactly one permitted action object on the final line; include no explanatory prose or other content. Treat the action protocol as a hard output constraint, including after demos and incorrect feedback.",
   "newText": "1. Use only the current instance's grid, demos, feedback, and counters; treat instances as independent and do not inspect other task artifacts. Compare every input/output cell, dimensions, preserved regions, and changes. For divider-panel transformations use `arc-divider-recolor`; for rectangular frames/fields use `arc-rectangle-noise`. An unchanged demo constrains only that example, not the general rule or test output. If the test lacks a defining structure apparent in demos, do not force it into that task family or guess a split; request a demo to establish applicability.\n2. When competing rules remain, request the demo most likely to distinguish them; track request cost and caps. Do not infer precise mappings from resemblance, color histograms, or stochastic-looking areas. If output contains apparently random/permuted content with no rule for exact values, samples do not license guessing. Separate fixed structure from undetermined cells; submit only if the scoring task permits a valid constrained completion, otherwise use remaining requests to investigate structural relations rather than invent a seed.\n3. Before submitting, require one rule to explain every changed and preserved cell in all demos. Construct exact dimensions and values; mechanically count rows and cells per row, compare non-background coordinates to the input, and check each value against the rule. For sparse grids, enumerate marked coordinates row by row before construction. After incorrect feedback, discard the proposed output; binary rejection does not identify the error. Request a discriminating demo instead of cycling guesses. Track attempt caps/costs; `finish` only once the instance is over.\n4. Every response must end with exactly one permitted action object and no explanatory prose or other content, including after demos and incorrect feedback."
  }
 ]
}
